Keynote talks Takeaways
@KaiqingZhang : Towards Principled Decision-Making and Interactive LLM Agents
Kaiqing Zhang emphasized that if we want LLMs to function as real agents capable of reasoning, adapting, and interacting strategically, we must fundamentally rethink how they learn. Instead of treating LLMs as static next-token predictors, he argued that we should draw inspiration from established principles in decision making such as regret minimization, structured exploration, coordination theory, and multi-agent learning.
He highlighted that today’s LLMs often behave reactively without the iterative feedback loops or strategic adaptation that define genuine agentic behavior. The field is still in its early stages, and the most impactful progress will come from identifying the right underlying principles rather than assuming that existing architectures will scale into agenthood. A full video of his talk will be posted on the MindGames website soon.
@jaseweston : Self-challenging Agents Can Improve Their Own Reasoning
Jason Weston discussed the promise and pitfalls of allowing AI systems to train themselves through self-rewarding and self-challenging pipelines. While these approaches can be extremely powerful, they also suffer from hallucination and information asymmetry: the model that generates the task is also the model that attempts to solve and evaluate it, meaning errors compound silently.
In their recent work SPICE, his team introduced a grounded challenger to stabilize this loop and reduce the failure modes. Jason emphasized that the ultimate goal is not only stronger autonomous self-training but also sustained AI and human co-improvement, where humans guide and validate the AI’s learning trajectory. His recent position paper, which expands on these ideas, is available here:
@salahalzubi401: ROMA: Recursive Open Meta Agents for Scalable Multi-Agent Systems
Salaheddin introduced ROMA as an open-source framework that makes it easy to build smarter, more capable AI agents by breaking big tasks into smaller, manageable pieces and solving them step by step. Rather than relying on one large model to do everything, ROMA coordinates a team of specialized mini-agents that plan, execute, and combine results in a clear and interpretable way. This approach makes complex, long-horizon tasks more reliable, more flexible, and far easier to scale, since different models and tools can be plugged in wherever they fit best. At its core, ROMA is built on a simple but powerful insight: better agents come from structure, collaboration, and transparency, not from forcing a single model to handle everything.
@jeffclune : Self-improving AI via Open-Ended Algorithms
Jeff Clune’s key message is that the fastest path to truly intelligent, self-improving AI comes from embracing quality diversity, open-endedness, and AI-generating algorithms. Quality diversity methods explore broadly, discover unexpected stepping stones, and switch goals to escape local optima. Open-ended algorithms build on this by endlessly producing new skills, challenges, and innovations, much like natural evolution and human culture. Together, these ideas form AI-generating algorithms that can automatically create increasingly capable AI systems and may offer the most promising route toward AGI. Through concepts like OMNI and Darwin-complete search spaces, Jeff Clune argues that we can construct never-ending learning environments and even automate aspects of scientific discovery, enabling recursively self-improving AI. His full talk can be found at

